% ============================================================
% Introduction
% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:synth-intro}

Program synthesis is the problem of automatically constructing a program that
satisfies a given specification.  Unlike program analysis, which extracts
information from existing programs, synthesis \emph{constructs} programs from
scratch---or, more precisely, from a specification that may take many forms:
input--output examples, logical formulae, demonstrations, sketches with holes,
or natural-language descriptions.

The dream of program synthesis is as old as computer science itself.  Alan
Turing~\cite{turing1950computing} speculated about machines that could write
programs, and Alan Biermann's early work on heuristic search for programs dates
to the 1970s.  However, it is only in the past two decades that program synthesis
has become a practical technology, driven by advances in constraint solving,
search algorithms, and machine learning.

Modern synthesis tools can generate sorting algorithms from a few
input--output pairs~\cite{gulwani2011program}, synthesize bit-manipulation
routines that outperform human-written code
(superoptimization)~\cite{masselin2013synthesis}, and automatically repair bugs
in large software systems~\cite{goues2016program}.

In this chapter, we develop the theory and practice of program synthesis.
We begin with the formal problem statement (\cref{sec:synth-problem}), then
explore the major synthesis paradigms: inductive (\cref{sec:synth-inductive}),
deductive (\cref{sec:synth-deductive}), and sketch-based
(\cref{sec:synth-sketch}).  We examine the CEGIS loop
(\cref{sec:synth-cegis}), enumerative search (\cref{sec:synth-enumerative}),
and conclude with applications to superoptimization and program repair
(\cref{sec:synth-applications}).

% ============================================================
% Synthesis as Search
% ============================================================
\section{Synthesis as Search}\label{sec:synth-problem}

\subsection{The Synthesis Problem}\label{sec:synth-formal}

At its most general, program synthesis is the problem of finding a program in a
given language that satisfies a given specification.

\begin{definition}[Synthesis Problem]\label{def:synth-problem}
Given:
\begin{itemize}
\item A programming language $\mathcal{L}$ (defining the space of candidate programs).
\item A specification $\varphi$ (defining the desired behavior).
\end{itemize}
Find: A program $p \in \mathcal{L}$ such that $p$ satisfies $\varphi$, or report
that no such program exists.
\end{definition}

The difficulty of synthesis depends critically on the \emph{form} of the
specification $\varphi$ and the \emph{size} of the language $\mathcal{L}$.
Different specification forms lead to fundamentally different synthesis algorithms.

\subsection{Specification Forms}\label{sec:synth-specs}

\begin{definition}[Specification Types]\label{def:spec-types}
Common forms of specifications include:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Input--output examples:} $\varphi = \{(x_1, y_1), \ldots, (x_n, y_n)\}$
      where $p(x_i) = y_i$ for all $i$.
\item \textbf{Logical specification:} $\varphi$ is a formula in a specification
      language, typically a pre/post-condition pair $(\mathit{Pre}, \mathit{Post})$.
\item \textbf{Natural language:} $\varphi$ is a description in natural language.
\item \textbf{Demonstration:} $\varphi$ is a sequence of actions (e.g., keystrokes).
\item \textbf{Sketch with holes:} $\varphi$ is a partial program with
      unspecified components.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Search Space}\label{sec:synth-space}

The language $\mathcal{L}$ defines the space of candidate programs.  In practice,
synthesis tools restrict $\mathcal{L}$ in various ways:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Grammar-based:} $\mathcal{L}$ is defined by a context-free grammar
      of program expressions.  This is the most common approach for
      domain-specific synthesis.
\item \textbf{Component-based:} $\mathcal{L}$ consists of programs built from a
      fixed set of components (functions, operators) combined using a fixed set
      of combinators.
\item \textbf{Bounded:} $\mathcal{L}$ is restricted to programs of size at most
      $k$ (measured in AST nodes, instructions, or some other metric).
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Program Space]\label{def:program-space}
A \emph{program space} is a tuple $(\mathcal{G}, \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{K})$ where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathcal{G}$ is a set of \emph{generators} (producing values from inputs).
\item $\mathcal{C}$ is a set of \emph{combinators} (combining values).
\item $\mathcal{K}$ is a set of \emph{constraints} on the structure of programs.
\end{itemize}
The language $\mathcal{L}$ consists of all programs constructible from
$\mathcal{G}$ and $\mathcal{C}$ satisfying $\mathcal{K}$.
\end{definition}

% ============================================================
% Inductive Synthesis
% ============================================================
\section{Inductive Synthesis}\label{sec:synth-inductive}

\subsection{Synthesis from Examples}\label{sec:synth-examples}

Inductive synthesis constructs a program from a finite set of input--output
examples.  The key challenge is that any finite set of examples is consistent
with infinitely many programs; the synthesis algorithm must find the
\emph{simplest} or \emph{most natural} program consistent with the examples.

\begin{definition}[Inductive Synthesis]\label{def:inductive-synth}
Given a set of examples $E = \{(x_1, y_1), \ldots, (x_n, y_n)\}$ and a
language $\mathcal{L}$, find $p \in \mathcal{L}$ such that:
\[
\forall i.\; p(x_i) = y_i
\]
and $p$ is minimal according to some size metric $|\cdot|$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Version-Space Algebra}\label{sec:synth-version-space}

The version space approach~\cite{mitchell1977version} maintains a set of
candidate programs consistent with the examples seen so far.  Each new example
prunes the set.

\begin{definition}[Version Space]\label{def:version-space}
The \emph{version space} $V$ for a language $\mathcal{L}$ and example set $E$ is:
\[
V(E) = \{p \in \mathcal{L} \mid \forall (x, y) \in E.\; p(x) = y\}
\]
\end{definition}

The version space can be represented compactly using a lattice structure.
The \emph{version-space algebra}~\cite{mitchell1977version} provides operations
for combining version spaces:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Intersection:} $V_1 \cap V_2$ is the set of programs consistent
      with both example sets.
\item \textbf{Union:} $V_1 \cup V_2$ is the set of programs consistent with
      either example set.
\item \textbf{Join:} The set of programs formed by combining programs from
      $V_1$ and $V_2$ using a combinator.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Decision-Tree Based Synthesis}\label{sec:synth-decision-tree}

For synthesis of recursive functions over algebraic data types, decision-tree
based approaches~\cite{feser2015synthesizing} learn a function by constructing
a decision tree that maps inputs to outputs.  Each internal node tests a
predicate; each leaf produces a value.  The synthesis algorithm searches for
the smallest decision tree consistent with the examples.

\subsection{Magic Values and Abstraction}\label{sec:synth-abstraction}

A key technique in inductive synthesis is \emph{abstraction}: replacing concrete
values in examples with symbolic variables.  For example, given the examples
$\mathit{reverse}([1,2,3]) = [3,2,1]$ and $\mathit{reverse}([4,5]) = [5,4]$,
the synthesizer abstracts the concrete list elements to discover that the
function reverses the order of elements regardless of their values.

