% Deductive Synthesis
% ============================================================
\section{Deductive Synthesis}\label{sec:synth-deductive}

\subsection{Synthesis from Logical Specifications}\label{sec:synth-logical}

Deductive synthesis constructs programs from logical specifications using proof
techniques.  The idea is to \emph{prove} that a program satisfying the
specification exists, and to \emph{extract} the program from the proof.

This approach is deeply connected to the Curry--Howard correspondence
(\cref{ch:proof-theory}): a program is a proof, and a specification is a
theorem.  Synthesizing a program from a specification is equivalent to proving
a theorem and extracting its witness.

\begin{definition}[Deductive Synthesis]\label{def:deductive-synth}
Given a specification $\forall \vec{x}.\; \exists \vec{y}.\; \varphi(\vec{x}, \vec{y})$,
deductive synthesis constructs a proof of this formula and extracts a program
$p$ such that $\forall \vec{x}.\; \varphi(\vec{x}, p(\vec{x}))$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Refinement Calculus}\label{sec:synth-refinement}

The refinement calculus~\cite{back1998refinement,morgan1990programming} provides
a framework for deductive synthesis by stepwise refinement.  Starting from an
abstract specification, the developer (or an automated tool) applies refinement
rules to produce increasingly concrete programs.

\begin{definition}[Refinement]\label{def:refinement}
A program $S_2$ \emph{refines} a program $S_1$, written $S_1 \sqsubseteq S_2$,
if every behavior of $S_2$ is an allowed behavior of $S_1$.  In the setting of
pre/post-conditions:
\[
(\mathit{Pre}_1, \mathit{Post}_1) \sqsubseteq (\mathit{Pre}_2, \mathit{Post}_2)
\iff
(\mathit{Pre}_2 \implies \mathit{Pre}_1) \land (\mathit{Pre}_2 \implies (\mathit{Post}_1 \implies \mathit{Post}_2))
\]
\end{definition}

Key refinement rules include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Assignment introduction:} Refine $\{P\}\ \mathbf{skip}\ \{Q\}$ to
      $\{P\}\ x := e\ \{Q\}$ when $P \implies Q[x \mapsto e]$.
\item \textbf{Condition introduction:} Refine a specification to an if-then-else
      by splitting the precondition.
\item \textbf{Loop introduction:} Refine a specification to a while loop by
      providing a loop invariant and variant.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Program Extraction from Proofs}\label{sec:synth-extraction}

Proof assistants such as Coq~\cite{coq2020}, Isabelle/HOL~\cite{nipkow2002},
and Agda~\cite{norell2007agda} support \emph{program extraction}: extracting
executable programs from constructive proofs.  Under the Curry--Howard
correspondence, a proof of $\forall x{:}A.\, \exists y{:}B.\, P(x,y)$ contains
within it a function $f : A \to B$ that computes the witness $y$ from $x$.

\begin{insight}
Deductive synthesis produces programs that are \emph{correct by construction}:
the program is guaranteed to satisfy its specification because it was derived
from a proof of the specification.  This is the strongest guarantee possible,
but it comes at the cost of requiring detailed specifications and significant
user interaction (or powerful automation) to construct the proof.
\end{insight}

