% Sketch-Based Synthesis
% ============================================================
\section{Sketch-Based Synthesis}\label{sec:synth-sketch}

\subsection{The Sketch Approach}\label{sec:synth-sketch-approach}

The Sketch system~\cite{solar2006sketch,solar2013synthesis} pioneered the idea
of \emph{sketch-based synthesis}: the programmer provides a \emph{sketch}---a
partial program with \emph{holes} (unknown values to be filled in)---and the
synthesizer completes the sketch by finding values for the holes that satisfy
the specification.

\begin{definition}[Sketch]\label{def:sketch}
A \emph{sketch} is a program $S(h_1, \ldots, h_n)$ containing \emph{holes}
$h_1, \ldots, h_n$---syntactic placeholders for unknown values.  Given a
specification $\varphi$, the synthesis problem is to find values
$v_1, \ldots, v_n$ such that $\varphi(S(v_1, \ldots, v_n))$ holds.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Sketch Example]\label{ex:sketch}
Consider the task of synthesizing an efficient implementation of $x \times 15$
using only shifts and additions.  A sketch might be:
\begin{verbatim}
  int multiply15(int x) {
    return (x << ??) - x;
  }
\end{verbatim}
The hole \texttt{??} represents an unknown shift amount.  The synthesizer finds
that \texttt{??} $= 4$ works, yielding:
\begin{verbatim}
  int multiply15(int x) {
    return (x << 4) - x;  // = 16x - x = 15x
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Hole Types}\label{sec:synth-hole-types}

Sketch supports several types of holes:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Integer holes (\texttt{??}):} Unknown integer constants.
\item \textbf{Expression holes:} Unknown expressions drawn from a grammar.
\item \textbf{Generator holes:} Unknown values drawn from a user-defined set
      (e.g., a set of available library functions).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{CEGIS for Sketches}\label{sec:synth-sketch-cegis}

The Sketch system uses the CEGIS (Counterexample-Guided Inductive Synthesis)
loop to solve sketches.  The algorithm is described in \cref{sec:synth-cegis}.

