\section{Enumerative Synthesis}\label{sec:synth-enumerative}

\subsection{Bottom-Up Enumeration}\label{sec:synth-enumeration}

Enumerative synthesis generates candidate programs in order of increasing size
and tests each against the specification.  The key optimization is to enumerate
programs \emph{bottom-up}---generating all expressions of size 1, then size 2,
and so on---and to prune the search space using various techniques.

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Bottom-Up Enumerative Synthesis}\label{alg:enum-synth}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Component set $\mathcal{C}$, examples $E$, size bound $k$
\Ensure Program $p$ consistent with $E$, or \textsc{None}
\State $L[0] \gets \{\text{input variables}\}$
\For{$s = 1$ to $k$}
  \State $L[s] \gets \emptyset$
  \For{each $c \in \mathcal{C}$ of arity $a$}
    \For{each $(e_1, \ldots, e_a) \in L[i_1] \times \cdots \times L[i_a]$ where $\sum i_j = s - 1$}
      \State $e \gets c(e_1, \ldots, e_a)$
      \If{$e$ is consistent with $E$}
        \State \Return $e$
      \EndIf
      \If{$e$ is not dominated by any existing expression}
        \State $L[s] \gets L[s] \cup \{e\}$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\State \Return \textsc{None}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Observational Equivalence}\label{sec:synth-obs-equiv}

A crucial optimization in enumerative synthesis is pruning based on
\emph{observational equivalence}.  Two expressions are observationally equivalent
(on the given examples) if they produce the same outputs for all example inputs.
Only one representative of each equivalence class need be kept.

\begin{definition}[Observational Equivalence]\label{def:obs-equiv}
Two expressions $e_1$ and $e_2$ are \emph{observationally equivalent} with
respect to example set $E$ if:
\[
\forall (x, y) \in E.\; e_1(x) = e_2(x)
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Completeness of Observational Pruning]\label{thm:obs-pruning}
If a program $p$ is consistent with $E$, then there exists a program $p'$ that
is observationally equivalent to $p$ on $E$, has size $|p'| \leq |p|$, and
would not be pruned by observational equivalence pruning.
\end{theorem}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Meeting in the Middle on Output Vectors}

Observational signatures can do more than eliminate duplicate expressions.
For a sketch whose root is addition, they can turn a search over pairs
into a lookup. This is useful when the two holes range over different
grammars: build an index for one grammar and look for the complement
of each signature produced by the other.

\textbf{Contract.} Given finite candidate lists \(L,R\) for a sketch
\(l+r\), and input--output examples with target vector \(t\), return a
pair satisfying every example or report no pair. Terms are deterministic
and total over the examples, with exact integer addition. The objective
is feasibility, not minimal cost, and there are no constraints coupling
the holes except the output equations. Signatures are components in the
same fixed order of example inputs.

\begin{sudocode}[Index the right-hand hole]
\Let{H}{\text{empty map}}
\For{each term $r$ in $R$}
  \Let{v}{\Call{Signature}{r}}
  \To{One witness suffices for this fixed example set.}
  \If{$v$ is absent from $H$}
    \Let{H[v]}{r}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\For{each term $l$ in $L$}
  \To{Compute the only right signature that can work.}
  \Let{w}{t-\Call{Signature}{l}}
  \If{$w$ is present in $H$}
    \State \Return $l+H[w]$
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return no pair
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} Use inputs \(1,3\) with target vector \((5,9)\).
Let the left list be \(x,2x\), and the right list be \(1,3,x\).
The right index contains \((1,1),(3,3),(1,3)\). Subtracting the
signature of \(x\) gives the missing key \((4,6)\); subtracting that
of \(2x\) gives \((3,3)\). The result \(2x+3\) produces \(5\)
and \(9\) as required.

The invariant is that every indexed vector has an actual witness and
every processed right candidate has its vector represented. After
evaluation, hashing and subtracting vectors of length \(m\) takes
expected \(O(m(|L|+|R|))\) unit-cost work, rather than comparing all
pairs. The first result need not be correct beyond the examples.
If a verifier adds a distinguishing input, rebuild the index with
longer signatures from the original candidate lists; discarded terms
can become distinguishable again.

\subsection{Type-Directed Synthesis}\label{sec:synth-type-directed}

Type information dramatically reduces the search space.  If the target program
has type $A \to B$, then every sub-expression must have a type that is
compatible with its context.  This eliminates many candidates before they are
even generated.

The \textsc{Myth} system~\cite{osera2016typing} and the \textsc{Synquid}
system~\cite{polikarpova2016program} use type-directed synthesis to generate
functional programs from refinement type specifications.

