\section{Applications}\label{sec:synth-applications}

\subsection{Superoptimization}\label{sec:synth-super}

\emph{Superoptimization}~\cite{masselin1987approach,denali2002} is the
application of program synthesis to generate optimal instruction sequences.
Given a target program $p$, a superoptimizer synthesizes a shorter or faster
program $p'$ that computes the same function.

\begin{definition}[Superoptimization]\label{def:super}
Given a program $p$ and a cost function $c : \mathcal{L} \to \mathbb{N}$,
superoptimization finds $p' \in \mathcal{L}$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\forall x.\; p'(x) = p(x)$ (functional equivalence), and
\item $c(p') \leq c(p'')$ for all $p''$ equivalent to $p$ (optimality).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

The \textsc{Souper} superoptimizer~\cite{souper2020} works at the LLVM IR level,
using an SMT solver to verify equivalence and an enumerative search to find
optimal instruction sequences.  It has discovered peephole optimizations that
were missed by LLVM's hand-written optimization passes.

\subsection{Program Repair}\label{sec:synth-repair}

\emph{Program repair}~\cite{goues2016program,weimer2009finding} uses synthesis
to automatically fix bugs in programs.  Given a buggy program $p$ and a
specification $\varphi$ (typically a test suite), the repair tool synthesizes a
patch $\delta$ such that $p[\delta]$ satisfies $\varphi$.

\begin{definition}[Program Repair]\label{def:repair}
Given a program $p$, a set of \emph{faulty locations} $L$, and a specification
$\varphi$ (test suite), program repair finds a patch $\delta$ mapping each
$\ell \in L$ to a new expression such that $p[\delta]$ passes all tests in $\varphi$.
\end{definition}

The \textsc{Angelix} repair tool~\cite{nguyen2016angelix} uses semantic
analysis and constraint solving to repair bugs in C and Java programs.  It
first identifies suspicious expressions using fault localization, then
synthesizes replacements using a CEGIS-like loop.

\subsection{Syntax-Guided Synthesis (SyGuS)}\label{sec:synth-sygus}

The SyGuS framework~\cite{alur2013syntax} standardizes the problem of
syntax-guided synthesis: synthesizing a program from a grammar and a logical
specification.  The SyGuS competition has driven significant progress in
synthesis algorithms.

\begin{grammar}{SyGuS}
  \mathit{Program} & \produces \mathit{Grammar} \times \mathit{Specification} \\
  \mathit{Grammar} & \produces \mathit{NonTerminal} \to \mathit{Expression} \mid \mathit{NonTerminal}\ \mathit{NonTerminal} \\
  \mathit{Specification} & \produces \forall \vec{x}.\; \mathit{Pre}(\vec{x}) \implies \mathit{Post}(\vec{x}, f(\vec{x}))
\end{grammar}

\subsection{Neural Program Synthesis}\label{sec:synth-neural}

Recent work has applied neural networks to program synthesis.  Systems such as
\textsc{RobustFill}~\cite{balog2017robustfill} and
\textsc{DeepCoder}~\cite{bunel2017deepcoder} use sequence-to-sequence models to
generate programs from input--output examples.  While these systems lack the
formal guarantees of constraint-based approaches, they can be remarkably
effective for certain domains (e.g., string manipulation, data transformation).

\begin{historical}
Program synthesis has undergone several renaissances.  The initial wave in the
1970s, led by Waldinger~\cite{waldinger1977constructing} and
Manna--Waldinger~\cite{manna1980deductive}, used deductive methods but was
limited by the power of available theorem provers.  The second wave in the 2000s,
led by Gulwani~\cite{gulwani2011automating} and
Solar-Lezama~\cite{solar2006sketch}, introduced constraint-based approaches and
the CEGIS loop.  The current wave, beginning around 2015, combines classical
techniques with machine learning, enabling synthesis from natural language and
demonstrations.
\end{historical}

