% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:synth-summary}

Program synthesis transforms the art of programming into a formal search problem.
By specifying \emph{what} a program should do rather than \emph{how} to do it,
synthesis tools can discover implementations that are correct by construction,
optimal according to a cost metric, or simply beyond what a human programmer
would write.

The field continues to evolve rapidly.  Key open challenges include:
\begin{itemize}
\item Scaling synthesis to larger programs and more complex specifications.
\item Combining the guarantees of constraint-based synthesis with the
      flexibility of neural approaches.
\item Developing specification languages that are expressive enough to capture
      real-world requirements but tractable enough for automated reasoning.
\item Integrating synthesis into the everyday workflow of software developers.
\end{itemize}

The monograph by Gulwani et al.~\cite{gulwani2017program} provides a
comprehensive treatment of the field, while the textbook by
Polikarpova~\cite{polikarpova2020synthesis} offers a pedagogical introduction.
